window.docContents[198].resolve({"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Hodograph-and-Local-Legendre-Transformations--Using-the-unknown-as-a-coordinate":{"contents":"In a planar flow, the velocity  is a function of position. If its\nJacobian is nonsingular at , nearby velocities distinguish nearby\npositions: we can use  as the independent variable and write\n. A coefficient that originally depended on the unknown velocity,\nsuch as , becomes the known function\n. The derivatives change too, and their inverse-matrix\nformula is what makes the transformed equations linear.\n\nFor a scalar graph, the useful new coordinate is its slope\n. The inverse slope map is itself a gradient, obtained from\n. This cancellation carries through to the\nminimal-surface equation, and it applies to a graph whose Hessian has one\npositive and one negative eigenvalue.\n\nCoordinates are numbered , as in the Lean statements.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods\u0009Hodograph and Local Legendre Transformations","header":"9.2.1.6.1. Using the unknown as a coordinate","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Hodograph-and-Local-Legendre-Transformations--Using-the-unknown-as-a-coordinate"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/A-heat-equation-residual/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--A-heat-equation-residual":{"contents":"Return to the separated heat profile from the parabolic chapter:\n\nIt keeps its sine shape while its amplitude decays. Differentiating once in\ntime and twice in space gives the same expression,\n\nso the heat-equation residual is zero. On a rod with endpoints at  and\n, the profile also vanishes at both ends. At time zero it is the sine\nprofile. We have checked the differential identity, the boundary values, and\nthe initial value separately. A uniqueness argument would tell us whether\nanother solution with the same data is possible in the chosen solution class.\n\nThe initial-value check is essential even when the residual is exactly zero.\nFor any constant , the profile\n has the same zero residual\nand homogeneous boundary values, but its initial value differs from the\nprescribed sine by . We use this simple example to keep track\nof what a calculation has established: an equation, its data, and a\nuniqueness or stability theorem each answer a different part of the PDE\nproblem.\n\nWe have already used this kind of calculation for harmonic polynomials,\ntransport, and the rotation and shear flows. The same\ncalculator supports those proofs. For this profile we can follow the\nderivatives by eye. Longer formulas involve the same rules, with more terms\nand more conditions to track. The cas tactics\nprove supported exact identities, derivatives, and antiderivatives; cega\nproves numerical bounds from exact rational interval computations or applies\nan existing certificate, such as a Taylor or quadrature certificate with its\nanalytic hypotheses.\n\nThe fixed-point chapter gives one\nquantitative connection. For a contraction  with constant \nand fixed point , a candidate  satisfies\n. A certified defect becomes a solution-error\nbound through this stability estimate. For a PDE, the norm, boundary\nconditions, and inverse-operator estimate in that argument come from the\nanalysis of the problem, not from the residual computation.\n\nFor a real residual , the certificate relation ResidualBound R Ω ε\nmeans  for every . At\n it gives the exact equation  on that domain.\nAt positive tolerance it gives uniform control of the residual.\n\nWe justify the exact transformations with the\nsoundness of normalization theorem and\nthe numerical bounds with the\nsoundness of interval evaluation theorem.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation","header":"10.3. A heat-equation residual","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/A-heat-equation-residual/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--A-heat-equation-residual"}});